1
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:07,680
- [Louis] There's so much kinda
of trans stuff going on now,

2
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,940
but it tends to all be white, you know?

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:12,970
and I don't really wanna
kind of support the idea

4
00:00:12,970 --> 00:00:15,950
that all trans folks, or all transsexuals

5
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:17,580
are all European-Americans.

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,010
- [Kylar] You know, we have gay TV now.

7
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:23,580
I was just watching it the other day

8
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:25,291
and they have tons of trans people,

9
00:00:25,291 --> 00:00:27,490
it's like, but none of color, any color.

10
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:29,877
So I just think it's important
that we get our stories told

11
00:00:29,877 --> 00:00:31,410
and our voices heard.

12
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:33,528
- [Nicholas] I hope that this film

13
00:00:33,528 --> 00:00:36,110
just really just get out there to where

14
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:38,979
it wouldn't be a question
like, or someone said,

15
00:00:38,979 --> 00:00:42,146
"I never heard of a trans guy before."

16
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:45,617
To where it'll just
be, "Oh yeah, you know,

17
00:00:45,617 --> 00:00:47,753
"he a T guy or whatever,"

18
00:00:47,753 --> 00:00:50,160
and put it on top that he's Black

19
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,530
You know.

20
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:52,623
It's like, wow.

21
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:56,480
(jazzy music)

22
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:07,110
- As a kid, I always
used to play Perry Mason.

23
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:08,340
I was weird kid, you know,

24
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,410
other people wanted to
play dolls or whatever.

25
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:11,920
I wanted to play Perry Mason.

26
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:14,638
So I would, and every Saturday,

27
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:17,330
after watching the
episodes of Perry Mason,

28
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:20,630
my bed, and my room
would be my little table

29
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:23,490
and I would, you know, do cases.

30
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:27,830
And so, and I mean, I probably
was four or five then.

31
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:30,803
So, and then I did that and
I really just loved that.

32
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:37,140
And then as I grew, this
activism part of me grew,

33
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,130
but it was because my parents

34
00:01:39,130 --> 00:01:41,660
were very active in the community.

35
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:45,310
For them, their activism was
through the American Legion,

36
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:49,094
because my parents, my father
was a World War II veteran,

37
00:01:49,094 --> 00:01:52,300
my mother 10 years younger than he,

38
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:54,700
and they've been lifelong sweethearts.

39
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:59,700
And since she was 14, through
their whole lives together,

40
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:02,840
so, but they were very
active in the community,

41
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,340
through the American
Legion, through the church,

42
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,623
you know, Second Baptist Church.

43
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:12,200
And so I saw that and went with them

44
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:15,990
to clean the graveyards, to
mow other people's lawns,

45
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:20,220
to do these things, and
also just their attitudes

46
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:22,980
of you stand up for what you believe in.

47
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,220
And both my parents were stand up

48
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:26,913
for what you believe in people,

49
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,200
that, that just propelled my advocacy.

50
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:36,200
And when my life unfolded,
it was so important to fight

51
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:40,360
for rights of just
human beings in general.

52
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:43,460
And, and of course that
is my love and my passion.

53
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:46,530
I worked in corporate
America and went to work

54
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:48,380
in corporate America after law school,

55
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:50,670
again, thinking I'm not
going to be the cliche,

56
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:53,310
the black civil rights lawyer,

57
00:02:53,310 --> 00:02:55,360
the black lesbian at the time,

58
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:57,730
'cause that was the title that I thought

59
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:00,223
was closest to who I was at the time.

60
00:03:02,352 --> 00:03:04,070
And so, I went to work
in this corporation,

61
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:05,300
gonna make lots of money.

62
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,240
So I was going to conform.

63
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,898
It was horrible because
then I had to dress

64
00:03:09,898 --> 00:03:14,898
or try to dress like they
wanted with hose and makeup.

65
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,400
And I don't know any people
that wear those things,

66
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,140
but it's quite costly
to buy makeup and hose

67
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:22,640
and all those things, and
then you have to get up

68
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:25,325
at the crack of dawn to get dressed.

69
00:03:25,325 --> 00:03:26,158
(audience laughing)

70
00:03:26,158 --> 00:03:28,150
So you can tell, I'm kinda poor at that,

71
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:29,620
which is why my head is shaved.

72
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:32,397
I've never been for getting up early, but,

73
00:03:32,397 --> 00:03:35,350
(audience laughing)
but at any rate.

74
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:37,180
I would just rip those clothes off,

75
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:39,810
as soon as I got in the
car, out of the work,

76
00:03:39,810 --> 00:03:42,440
in the parking lot, you
know, and I couldn't stand,

77
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,940
you know, just as soon
as I could get them off.

78
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,260
I mean, I'd just be undressing as soon

79
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,640
as I got to the parking lot,
'cause it was just horrible

80
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,610
and the makeup, the thought of makeup,

81
00:03:52,610 --> 00:03:56,960
and then I tried to use
as little as possible.

82
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:59,370
And luckily, my skin is sensitive,

83
00:03:59,370 --> 00:04:01,500
so I could use that as an excuse

84
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:05,020
to not wear makeup or what they expected.

85
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,150
And those dress codes held through,

86
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:09,450
really for most women into the '90s.

87
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:11,970
And in some places, depending
upon where you live,

88
00:04:11,970 --> 00:04:13,650
that's still a fight.

89
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:17,780
And so my girlfriend, every
day we'd get up and say,

90
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:21,120
okay, this is a uniform, to
get me dressed every day.

91
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,757
I mean, it was really a
psychological, it's like,

92
00:04:23,757 --> 00:04:26,190
okay, okay, and I mean,
I would put the stuff on

93
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:29,874
and I would be so uncomfortable
carrying the purse around.

94
00:04:29,874 --> 00:04:31,420
I'll never forget that.

95
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:33,420
I had a good buddy that I worked with,

96
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,380
and he would remember the purse

97
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:37,190
because I had never remembered the purse.

98
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:39,850
And he's like, God, you carry
that like a lunch bucket

99
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:41,390
and blah, blah, blah, blah.

100
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:43,960
And I'm like, dude, you know, whatever.

101
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:45,750
So I gave up the purse part,

102
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:49,787
but he said to me, when I
stopped trying to do that,

103
00:04:49,787 --> 00:04:52,277
"Thank God you stopped doing that.

104
00:04:52,277 --> 00:04:53,840
"You looked horrible."

105
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:56,500
And I said to him, "You're my,"

106
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:59,037
at the time, "very good friend.

107
00:04:59,037 --> 00:05:00,587
"Why didn't you tell me?

108
00:05:00,587 --> 00:05:05,110
"I expect my friends to tell
me the truth," and he says,

109
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:07,210
you know, he didn't want
to hurt my feelings.

110
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:09,330
It's just like, well, next
time, tell me the truth.

111
00:05:09,330 --> 00:05:10,920
And I did, I looked horrible.

112
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:12,050
And I liked to say, I looked

113
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:15,380
like a bad drag queen, and I did, so.

114
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:18,490
And I don't think I was fooling anybody

115
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:23,490
but myself because who I
was transcended the clothes.

116
00:05:25,268 --> 00:05:29,960
And eventually I got a new
boss who'd made my life hell

117
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,490
and I was outta there
after announcing transition

118
00:05:32,490 --> 00:05:36,260
in six months, six months,
it was like fast, boom.

119
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:37,820
And I had been a good employee.

120
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:40,660
I just didn't think that
company would ever do that.

121
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,370
Just Pollyanna, I did a good job.

122
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:45,710
Why would they wanna get rid of me?

123
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:50,110
Because we know that how you get promoted

124
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:52,830
is how you look, right?

125
00:05:52,830 --> 00:05:56,050
How we get elected or how we
do anything is how we look.

126
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:58,340
So if we're treated differently,

127
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:01,920
and I can always analogize
this part of me to race,

128
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:04,320
where a lot of times
we're treated differently

129
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:07,570
because we were a certain race,
that really ostracizes us,

130
00:06:07,570 --> 00:06:09,530
it doesn't really help or benefit us

131
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:14,023
in any realm of the imagination
and makes matters worse.

132
00:06:15,977 --> 00:06:20,240
That's the whole dress thing
that I got on at Harvard,

133
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:22,600
because I'm really passionate about that,

134
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,250
about people feeling
like they can go to work

135
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:28,130
and be comfortable, and
not have an expectation

136
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:32,050
of having to be a certain
way or to be forced

137
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:36,970
to be feminized or, or
gendered or whatever.

138
00:06:36,970 --> 00:06:39,470
I started doing a lot of trans conferences

139
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:42,480
because I wanted to get legal information

140
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:44,663
out there about name changes,

141
00:06:45,650 --> 00:06:47,880
document changes, and so forth.

142
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,130
And people weren't doing that then.

143
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:52,740
And the big organization,
nobody was doing that then.

144
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:55,020
And me, and I would
meet a couple other guys

145
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:56,960
doing the trans circuit, so to speak,

146
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,120
and we'd go to conferences and doing that.

147
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:02,360
And that kinda started my
advocacy in the trans community,

148
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:06,100
just doing those Trans 101s
of how you get your documents,

149
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:07,370
where you get your documents,

150
00:07:07,370 --> 00:07:10,160
and how to navigate the process.

151
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:13,300
And that helped me come into, you know,

152
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,800
because then that allowed me to connect

153
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:18,490
to other trans people
and then to learn more

154
00:07:18,490 --> 00:07:20,440
about myself and embrace myself.

155
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,657
And one of the best things
were going to the conferences,

156
00:07:22,657 --> 00:07:24,530
And I remember True Spirit Conference

157
00:07:24,530 --> 00:07:26,480
when it was just like a few of us,

158
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,050
then a grew to larger and larger

159
00:07:29,050 --> 00:07:32,661
and a lot more trans people,
but just being able to go there

160
00:07:32,661 --> 00:07:36,150
and have somebody understand, you know,

161
00:07:36,150 --> 00:07:39,295
even without saying
what your experience is

162
00:07:39,295 --> 00:07:43,210
and just going, wow, look at
you, look at you, look at you.

163
00:07:43,210 --> 00:07:45,540
You know, it's like,
wow, you're buff there,

164
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:46,970
you're this you're that, you know,

165
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:48,670
all these things are happening.

166
00:07:48,670 --> 00:07:52,660
What are you doing about
scars on your chest?

167
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:55,570
You know, is my surgery comparable?

168
00:07:55,570 --> 00:07:57,780
Not meaning, you know, we're one ups man,

169
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:00,050
but did I get a good job or not?

170
00:08:00,050 --> 00:08:03,320
Does it look real and
showing each other our chest,

171
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:07,486
you know, talking about
issues, how do you date?

172
00:08:07,486 --> 00:08:09,960
You know, how do you dress?

173
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:13,550
And then it was nice
doing ones with the MTFs,

174
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:16,550
because they would like
exchange clothes with us

175
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:20,710
to help with teaching
guys how to tie ties.

176
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:24,410
And I think that was really a
part of my growing experience

177
00:08:24,410 --> 00:08:27,430
is going to the trans
conferences and connecting

178
00:08:27,430 --> 00:08:30,230
with other people, and
now they've grown up

179
00:08:30,230 --> 00:08:33,470
to such much, more advocacy,

180
00:08:33,470 --> 00:08:36,290
much more politically astute conferences,

181
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:37,740
which is a good thing.

182
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:41,917
But the beginning ones, when
it was just a few people

183
00:08:41,917 --> 00:08:45,070
coming together, were
some of the best ones.

184
00:08:45,070 --> 00:08:46,880
When I first started my activism,

185
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:50,930
I talked a lot about race and just being,

186
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:54,120
you know, gay, lesbian,
bisexual, transgender.

187
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:55,850
And when, particularly, when I would go

188
00:08:55,850 --> 00:08:59,430
to transgender conferences,
'cause I didn't want us to do

189
00:08:59,430 --> 00:09:03,680
what the gay movement had done,
but we do, we're doing it,

190
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:06,570
exactly the same thing,
'cause we are part of that

191
00:09:06,570 --> 00:09:09,510
and have become part of
that whole hierarchy.

192
00:09:09,510 --> 00:09:13,090
And you see it in the
movement, they will hire,

193
00:09:13,090 --> 00:09:15,780
and I got in trouble with saying
this in corporate America,

194
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:18,240
but they will hire a white person

195
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:22,160
over a qualified black person
and think nothing of it.

196
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:25,170
And they're, well, where can
we find qualified black people?

197
00:09:25,170 --> 00:09:26,150
Where are they at?

198
00:09:26,150 --> 00:09:29,520
Where are qualified Hispanic
people, Asian people,

199
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:32,100
when we're there, we're
there at the table,

200
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,730
we're visible, but we're looked over.

201
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:37,350
We're never considered as qualified.

202
00:09:37,350 --> 00:09:41,370
And then we don't get into
the decision making positions

203
00:09:41,370 --> 00:09:44,230
in the organizations that make decisions

204
00:09:44,230 --> 00:09:48,020
about the gay movement and the decisions

205
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:51,560
that are made in lobbying
for bills and legislation.

206
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,440
Sure, we get invited to the table,

207
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:56,120
one or two of us get invited to the table

208
00:09:56,120 --> 00:09:58,400
because a few people feel like, oh,

209
00:09:58,400 --> 00:09:59,860
well, we should look for parity,

210
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:03,350
but I don't feel like that's
a real concern in the,

211
00:10:03,350 --> 00:10:06,843
at large movement, if
you will, that it is,

212
00:10:06,843 --> 00:10:11,809
be diverse racially,
economically or otherwise,

213
00:10:11,809 --> 00:10:15,340
because no oppression
is greater than another.

214
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:18,884
And I get very angry about
that when I have some people,

215
00:10:18,884 --> 00:10:21,270
particularly people that are not of color,

216
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:25,140
try to call me on that because
there is a lot of energy

217
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:28,850
and anger around being
of color in this country.

218
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:30,410
And we have a right to own that.

219
00:10:30,410 --> 00:10:33,770
And I had somebody say, oh,
well, one's not any greater,

220
00:10:33,770 --> 00:10:35,800
well, no one isn't any
greater than the other,

221
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:39,390
but don't assume, you know my experience

222
00:10:39,390 --> 00:10:41,440
until you've walked in my shoes

223
00:10:43,641 --> 00:10:47,580
and don't dismiss it at all.

224
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:50,260
And I get angry because I feel
it's dismissed all the time.

225
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:52,810
And I don't mean to
personalize it just to me

226
00:10:52,810 --> 00:10:54,886
because I see it happen to my brothers

227
00:10:54,886 --> 00:10:59,520
and sisters within the
community as well, so.

228
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:02,380
But I think we get it from all sides.

229
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:06,083
And you know, in
employment, it's very clear,

230
00:11:08,180 --> 00:11:11,500
we are not white men, so it's very clear,

231
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:14,833
that hierarchy, and I
think pay is affected.

232
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,130
I work for a historically black college.

233
00:11:18,130 --> 00:11:19,990
So I think that's a
little bit differently,

234
00:11:19,990 --> 00:11:22,283
but I think race plays a part there.

235
00:11:25,070 --> 00:11:28,980
In any job environment where you interact

236
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,203
with people of other races and ethnicity.

237
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:36,610
I feel so marginalized a lot of times

238
00:11:36,610 --> 00:11:40,840
by people and things and
my life experience that,

239
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:44,500
yeah, it felt like a milestone
to be asked to go to Harvard

240
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:47,980
because people usually
diss my intelligence

241
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:49,700
on a daily basis.

242
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:54,700
They do do due to my race or
because I'm a transsexual.

243
00:11:54,770 --> 00:11:57,273
And so I'm discounted as a person.

244
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:01,273
It's hard for me when I practice, here,

245
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:08,060
in the city I live in, because
of the negativity of that.

246
00:12:08,310 --> 00:12:11,130
And one of my clients go
to court with me one day

247
00:12:11,130 --> 00:12:11,963
and he comes out, he goes,

248
00:12:11,963 --> 00:12:15,270
"I don't know how you stand
